  "drywall-repair-waltham": {
    "serviceSlug": "drywall-repair",
    "citySlug": "waltham",
    "heading": "Wall and Ceiling Repair in Waltham's Pre-War and Multi-Unit Housing",
    "lead": [
      "Lead paint is the first thing we plan around on a Waltham wall repair. Seventy percent of homes predate 1980, many of them under the pre-1978 lead rule, and the older Victorian and Colonial Revival houses usually have plaster on wood lath under many layers of paint. Opening a wall or sanding a patch in that setting creates dust that has to be contained. As an EPA Lead-Safe (RRP) certified firm, we set up plastic barriers, HEPA vacuums and a cleaning check before anyone moves back into the room.",
      "The other thing that sets Waltham apart is how people live: only 49 percent of units are owner-occupied, and 23 percent of housing is in two- to four-unit buildings. Water from an upstairs bath often shows up as a ceiling stain in the unit below, so the repair involves two households. Charles River humidity can also keep basement walls damp. We match plaster or skim coat as closely as we can, and we do not claim a repair on an old ceiling will be impossible to find."
    ],
    "planning": [
      {
        "title": "Fix the unit above first",
        "body": "If a stain came from upstairs, the leak has to be repaired in that unit before your ceiling is opened. The owners or tenants above should agree on access for a licensed plumber, so the patch below is not undone by a second drip."
      },
      {
        "title": "Give tenants notice of containment",
        "body": "Lead-safe work closes off part of a unit until cleanup is verified. Give tenants written notice of the dates, and clear furniture and belongings from the work area ahead of time so the plastic barriers can go up quickly on the first morning."
      },
      {
        "title": "Check basement walls in humid months",
        "body": "Drywall finished against a damp foundation can grow mold behind its paper face. Before a basement patch is replaced, see whether that wall stays wet through a humid summer, and deal with the moisture first so the new board lasts."
      }
    ],
    "faq": {
      "question": "Our landlord wants to skim-coat the old plaster. Is that safe with lead paint underneath?",
      "answer": "It can be, if the prep is done correctly. Skim coating itself covers the old paint, but scraping loose areas and sanding edges beforehand can release lead dust. Under the EPA RRP rule, that prep should be done by a certified firm with containment, restricted access and a thorough cleanup. We follow those steps on pre-1978 surfaces. Tenants should stay out of the work area until cleanup is done and the room is released."
    },
    "updated": "2026-09-30"
  },
