      "Roughly half of Watertown's homes are rented, and only 29 percent of the housing is single-family. That changes what interior painting looks like. A good share of the work is in units of two- and three-family houses, where rooms are painted between tenants or while someone is living there, and shared stairwells and hallways belong to everyone in the building. Scheduling around occupants, protecting common areas and keeping lead dust contained matter as much as the color choice, and often take more of the planning.",
      "The median home was built in 1945, right around the time New England builders were moving from plaster on wood lath to rock lath and early gypsum board. A single two-family can have original plaster in the front rooms and drywall in a later kitchen or rear addition, each needing different patching. With 79 percent of Watertown housing built before 1980, many of those older surfaces fall under the pre-1978 lead rule, so we follow EPA RRP practices when sanding or scraping. For turnover work, we agree on a set window for prep, paint and cleanup."
