  "interior-painting-winthrop": {
    "serviceSlug": "interior-painting",
    "citySlug": "winthrop",
    "heading": "Painting Plaster Interiors in Winthrop's 1930s Houses",
    "lead": [
      "The median Winthrop home was built in 1938, which in New England almost always means plaster on wood lath. Plaster from that era is hard and durable, but decades of settling leave cracks along ceilings and at door corners, and older calcimine or oil coats underneath can reject modern latex. We test for adhesion, repair cracks with mesh and setting compound, and use a bonding primer where the old surface calls for it. Trim from that period tends to have deeper profiles, so it takes more careful sanding and more time to cut in cleanly.",
      "Eighty-seven percent of Winthrop's housing was built before 1980, so lead paint is a real possibility on trim, windows and doors that have been painted many times. As an EPA Lead-Safe (RRP) certified firm, we test before disturbing those surfaces and set up containment when results call for it. Only 56 percent of homes are owner-occupied, so many interior jobs are in rented units. For landlords, we can work between tenants or coordinate access with occupants, and we plan around shared halls in two- and three-family buildings."
    ],
    "planning": [
      {
        "title": "Tap the walls for loose plaster",
        "body": "Press gently or tap along walls and ceilings. A hollow sound or slight give means plaster has let go of the lath. Those areas need securing with plaster washers or adhesive before painting, or new paint may crack along with them. Marking the spots with painter's tape saves time at the walkthrough."
      },
      {
        "title": "Coordinate tenant access early",
        "body": "If the unit is rented, set dates with the tenant before we schedule. Let them know which rooms will be closed and for how long. Clear access keeps the job moving and avoids reworking a room that was used too soon."
      },
      {
        "title": "Keep original trim if you can",
        "body": "Pre-war casings and baseboards are hard to replace with anything similar. If the trim is in reasonable shape, plan to strip only loose paint and repaint, rather than replacing it with thinner modern moldings. Replacement stock rarely matches the old profiles."
      }
    ],
    "faq": {
      "question": "The paint in our hallway flakes off in sheets. What causes that in an older house?",
      "answer": "Sheets of peeling paint in a house from the 1930s often mean latex was put directly over an old oil or calcimine coat without a bonding primer. The new layer never bonded, so it lifts away as a skin. The fix is to remove the loose layers, test for lead first, then prime with a product made to grip the old surface before finish coats. Humidity from a nearby bathroom can make it worse, so ventilation is worth checking too."
    },
    "updated": "2026-09-30"
  },
